<sling = 'http://sling.apache.org/jcr/sling/1.0'>
<rep = 'internal'>
<data = 'https://iap.uhndata.io/data/'>
<hist = 'https://iap.uhndata.io/hist/'>

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// History: the record of what happened to the content, kept under /History.
//
// TWO STORES, ONE RECORD. The *content* of a past state is a JCR version, in
// the repository's own version storage: a frozen node is exempt from
// referential integrity, keeps binaries by reference to the same blobs, and
// preserves the resource type, so past content can be read back through the
// ordinary model and serialization stack. What version storage cannot do is
// carry a word of our own -- an nt:version and its jcr:frozenNode are both
// entirely protected, and no property or mixin can be added to either, ever.
// Everything a reader needs to know *about* a change therefore lives here, and
// the two halves are joined by the version identifier in hist:Entry.snapshot.
//
// ONE COMMIT, ONE ACTION. An action is one event delivery -- one thing a user
// or a timer asked for -- and not one step of the workflow that carried it out.
// The engine runs a workflow to quiescence and commits it all at once, so a
// step has no separately committed existence and cannot be an audit fact. This
// is also what keeps the record stable when a definition is refactored: an
// action that retires one workflow version and activates another is one action
// whether the definition spends one service task on it or two.
//
// THE CAUSE IS ON THE ACTION, THE EFFECT IS ON THE ENTRY. One action affects
// any number of resources, each in its own way, so the cause is recorded once
// and each affected resource gets its own hist:Entry naming the part it played.
// Neither shape says enough alone: a list of touched identifiers cannot say
// which version was retired and which activated, and a record per resource
// cannot say that both happened together for one reason.
//
// NO VALUES. An entry says *which* properties changed, never what they changed
// to. That keeps the log small, keeps it readable at a glance, and says
// something the snapshot beside it cannot.
//
// Nothing here is versionable and /History is not under a versionable
// ancestor, so no on-parent-version attributes are needed in this file -- with
// the single exception of the hist:Watchable mixin at the bottom, which is
// added to content that *is* versionable, and says so where it is declared.
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The store root at /History, and every bucket under it.
//
// One type for both, because the layout is a prefix tree: actions are filed
// under <root>/<xx>/<yy>/<zz>/<action>, the buckets named after the leading
// characters of the action's own name, exactly as PrefixTree files them and as
// Oak files version histories. A flat parent is what this avoids -- a node with
// hundreds of thousands of children is one no repository browser can open and
// one the repository itself starts warning about.
//
// rep:AccessControllable because the ACL that matters is placed here: nobody
// reads this store directly. It is read by a service user, which adapts what it
// finds into what the person asking is allowed to see, so per-audience
// visibility is a decision made when the history is served rather than one
// expressed in permissions.
[hist:Log] > sling:Folder, rep:AccessControllable
  // Attributes:

  // We can query the record
  query

  // Properties:

  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "hist/Log" mandatory autocreated protected

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Child nodes:

  // A bucket of the prefix tree, holding further buckets or the actions
  // themselves
  + * (hist:Log) = hist:Log

  // An action, at the bottom of the prefix tree
  + * (hist:Action) = hist:Action

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One action: one thing that was asked for, and everything that followed from
// it in the single commit that carried it out.
//
// data:Content > sling:Folder > nt:folder > nt:hierarchyNode > mix:created, so
// jcr:created and jcr:createdBy are autocreated by the repository. jcr:created
// is when the action happened; jcr:createdBy is the service user that recorded
// it, which is why the person responsible is a property of its own below.
[hist:Action] > data:Content
  // Attributes:

  // We can query the record
  query

  // Properties:

  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "hist/Action" mandatory autocreated protected

  // Who is answerable for this. Deliberately not jcr:createdBy: every write
  // here is made by a service user, because the engine writes as itself and
  // the person acting often holds no more than read access on what they
  // changed. Must be a canonical user id, as the repository spells it, and not
  // the spelling the person typed at login -- those differ, and two spellings
  // of one person is a record that cannot be totalled.
  - actor (STRING) mandatory

  // Set when somebody acted for somebody else -- a delegate, or the system on
  // a person's behalf. `actor` stays whoever really did it.
  - onBehalfOf (STRING)

  // What was asked for, in the platform's own vocabulary: "submit",
  // "activateVersion". The one property a reader can group by without knowing
  // any workflow definition.
  - operation (STRING) mandatory

  // The workflow instance this action moved, and the exact definition version
  // that decided what it meant. Identifiers as STRING rather than REFERENCE,
  // like every other pointer in this file: version storage is exempt from
  // referential integrity but ordinary content is not, so a REFERENCE here
  // would make the record pin what it describes and refuse to let anybody
  // delete it. The record has to be able to outlive its subject.
  - workflowInstance (STRING)
  - workflowVersion (STRING)

  // Which node of the definition was being performed, and its label as the
  // definition read at the time. The label is copied rather than looked up for
  // the same reason a task copies its own: the reader may not be able to read
  // the definition at all, and the definition may since have been reworded.
  - activityId (STRING)
  - activityLabel (STRING)

  // The task somebody completed to cause this, the decision they took, and what
  // they said about it. Nothing routes on the note, which is exactly why it has
  // to be recorded rather than left to be remembered.
  - taskInstance (STRING)
  - outcome (STRING)
  - outcomeNote (STRING)

  // The name of the event that was delivered
  - event (STRING)

  // For an action no workflow caused -- a scheduled sweep, a migration, an
  // import. Not every change has an actor who chose it, and a record that can
  // only describe user actions quietly omits the rest.
  - component (STRING)

  // The action this one was performed as part of, once BPMN subprocesses exist.
  // One property and no mechanism: the alternative is discovering later that
  // nesting cannot be expressed without rewriting the store.
  - parentAction (STRING)

  // Whether every snapshot this action intended was taken. It exists because
  // they cannot all be taken with it: a check-in refuses to run while the
  // session has pending changes and commits by itself, so the snapshots of an
  // action affecting several resources are necessarily separate commits made
  // after this record. Until this says otherwise, an entry with no `snapshot`
  // may simply not have got one yet; once it does, an entry without one never
  // wanted one.
  - complete (BOOLEAN) = false mandatory autocreated

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Child nodes:

  // What the action did, one child per resource it affected
  + * (hist:Entry) = hist:Entry

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What one action did to one resource.
//
// Named after the subject's identifier, which is what makes "what did this
// action do to that resource" a path rather than a search, and lets the
// repository itself refuse a second entry for the same resource in the same
// action.
[hist:Entry] > data:Content
  // Attributes:

  // We can query the record: this is how the history of one resource is
  // gathered, since the entries are filed under their action rather than under
  // their subject
  query

  // Properties:

  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "hist/Entry" mandatory autocreated protected

  // Which resource this is about, by identifier. A STRING for the reason given
  // on the action's own pointers, and the reason applies most sharply here.
  - subject (STRING) mandatory

  // Where it was and what it was at the time. Both are copies, and both are
  // needed: a path moves when content is archived or restored, and a type is
  // how an entry stays legible after its subject no longer exists to be asked.
  - subjectPath (STRING) mandatory
  - subjectType (STRING) mandatory

  // The part this resource played in the action -- "submitted", "retired",
  // "activated", "flagged". The property that carries the meaning: an action
  // affecting two resources usually did two different things, and without this
  // the record degenerates into a list of what it touched.
  //
  // Deliberately not constrained to a value list. The vocabulary belongs to the
  // workflow definitions, which grow their own operations, and a CND constraint
  // here would fail the commit that records a change rather than the change
  // itself -- recording must never be what stops somebody working.
  - role (STRING) mandatory

  // Which properties changed, by name. Never their values: see the header.
  - changes (STRING) multiple

  // The identifier of the JCR version holding this resource's content as it was
  // after the action -- the join to the other store. Absent when this action
  // took no snapshot of this resource, which is the normal case: the log
  // records every action, snapshots are taken where the process says a
  // milestone is.
  - snapshot (STRING)

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Child nodes:

  // Anything said about this afterwards
  + * (hist:Annotation) = hist:Annotation

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Something somebody said about a past change, after it happened.
//
// This is the whole answer to a version being unannotatable. A frozen node
// cannot be given a property, so a later decision -- "this is the revision the
// approval was granted on", "this figure was wrong and here is why" -- has
// nowhere to live in version storage. It lives here instead, appended and never
// replaced, in the shape a triage decision on a recorded error already uses.
[hist:Annotation] > data:Content
  // Attributes:

  // We can query annotations
  query

  // Properties:

  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "hist/Annotation" mandatory autocreated protected

  // Who said it. A canonical user id, as on the action.
  - author (STRING) mandatory

  // What they said
  - note (STRING) mandatory

  // Optionally, what it amounts to: a short machine-readable verdict beside the
  // words, for annotations that are decisions rather than remarks
  - resolution (STRING)

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Marks content that remembers who has looked at it.
//
// A mixin, and added to the content rather than declared on data:Entity,
// because the node type registration order forbids the alternative: these types
// build on data:Content, so a container of them named from entities.cnd would
// need this bundle to start before the data model that it is built on.
//
// IGNORE on-parent-version, and it is load-bearing. Without it every marker
// would be copied into every snapshot, so a snapshot would differ from the one
// before it because somebody opened the page, and restoring an old revision
// would tell people they have not seen what they have.
[hist:Watchable]
  // Attributes:

  mixin

  // Child nodes:

  + hist:lastSeen (hist:LastSeen) = hist:LastSeen AUTOCREATED IGNORE

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The markers on one piece of content, one per person who has looked at it.
//
// Under the content and not under the person, on the cardinalities: a busy
// reviewer accumulates a marker for every item they ever open, while an item is
// only ever looked at by a handful of people. The lookup that actually happens
// -- what has this person seen of this item -- is a path either way, so what is
// left to decide by is which parent grows without bound. The dashboard does not
// enumerate a person's markers: it starts from what is assigned to them and
// asks about each.
[hist:LastSeen] > data:Content
  // Attributes:

  // We can query the markers
  query

  // Properties:

  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "hist/LastSeen" mandatory autocreated protected

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Child nodes:

  // One marker per viewer, named after their canonical user id
  + * (hist:Marker) = hist:Marker

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// How much of one item's history one person has seen.
[hist:Marker] > data:Content
  // Attributes:

  // We can query the markers
  query

  // Properties:

  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "hist/Marker" mandatory autocreated protected

  // When they last looked. This, and not a version, is what answers "has
  // anything happened since?": snapshots are only taken at milestones, so the
  // newest thing to have happened to an item usually has no version of its own,
  // and a marker naming a version could not see it. Compared against the most
  // recent action naming this item.
  - seenAt (DATE) mandatory

  // Which action they had seen up to, when it is worth knowing precisely
  - seenAction (STRING)

  // The newest snapshot they had seen, which is what a "what changed since I
  // looked" comparison has to start from. Necessarily coarser than `seenAt`:
  // there may be no snapshot as recent as the moment they looked.
  - seenSnapshot (STRING)

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured
